From f77b6f4ef2904f314defbca0463d7d02d1732fcd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Damien Dusha <Damien Dusha>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 22:16:41 +1000
Subject: [PATCH] config: store the logid flag, and derive CONFIG_LOG_ALL from
 the flags

Two defects that mask each other, so both have to move together.

ptp_store_config() ends the assignment a line early:

    CONFIG_ADD_LOGGING(S.logging.transmission, CONFIG_LOG_TRANSMISSION);
    CONFIG_ADD_LOGGING(S.logging.logid, CONFIG_LOG_LOGID);

The second line is a discarded expression statement, so the logid bit is
never written to the record.

Fixing only that is worse than leaving it. CONFIG_LOG_LOGID took bit 0x80
while CONFIG_LOG_ALL stayed a literal 0x7F, and ptp_load_config() rejects the
whole record on any bit outside the mask:

    invalid |= pConfig->logging & (~CONFIG_LOG_ALL);
    ...
    if (invalid) { MSG("The retained flexPTP configuration got corrupted, loading aborted!\n"); return; }

So a record saved with logging IDs enabled is discarded in its entirety --
profile, clock offset, priority1 and priority2 with it -- and the user is told
their configuration is corrupt.

Measured on a NUCLEO-H563ZI through a CLI hook that round-trips the record
(this port defines no PTP_CONFIG_PTR, so nothing reaches the path on its own).
All logging flags on, store, all flags off, load:

  before             stored 0x3f, logid bit MISSING, load accepted
  semicolon only     stored 0xbf, logid bit present, load REJECTED as
                     corrupted -- nothing restored, not one flag
  both               stored 0xbf, logid bit present, load accepted, every
                     flag restored

CONFIG_LOG_ALL is now derived from the flags rather than written as a literal,
because the failure mode of a stale literal is not "the new flag is ignored",
it is "the user's whole configuration is thrown away".

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/flexptp/config.c | 11 +++++++++--
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/flexptp/config.c b/src/flexptp/config.c
index f62787b..df91bfe 100644
--- a/src/flexptp/config.c
+++ b/src/flexptp/config.c
@@ -20,7 +20,14 @@
 #define CONFIG_LOG_BMCA (0x20)         ///< Peek BMCA state changes
 #define CONFIG_LOG_TRANSMISSION (0x40) ///< Log transmission events
 #define CONFIG_LOG_LOGID (0x80)        ///< Print log IDs
-#define CONFIG_LOG_ALL (0x7F)          ///< All logging options packed
+/* Derived from the flags rather than written as a literal. ptp_load_config() rejects the whole
+   record if any bit outside this mask is set, so a literal that falls behind the flag list does
+   not merely ignore the new flag -- it discards the user's entire retained configuration and
+   reports it as corrupted. That is what a bare 0x7F did once CONFIG_LOG_LOGID took bit 0x80. */
+#define CONFIG_LOG_ALL                                                        \
+    (CONFIG_LOG_DEF | CONFIG_LOG_INFO | CONFIG_LOG_CORR |                     \
+     CONFIG_LOG_TIMESTAMPS | CONFIG_LOG_LOCKED | CONFIG_LOG_BMCA |            \
+     CONFIG_LOG_TRANSMISSION | CONFIG_LOG_LOGID) ///< All logging options packed
 
 ///\cond 0
 #define CONFIG_ADD_LOGGING(c, f) (((c) ? (f) : 0))
@@ -37,7 +44,7 @@ void ptp_store_config(PtpConfig *pConfig) {
                        CONFIG_ADD_LOGGING(S.logging.timestamps, CONFIG_LOG_TIMESTAMPS) |
                        CONFIG_ADD_LOGGING(S.logging.locked, CONFIG_LOG_LOCKED) |
                        CONFIG_ADD_LOGGING(S.logging.bmca, CONFIG_LOG_BMCA) |
-                       CONFIG_ADD_LOGGING(S.logging.transmission, CONFIG_LOG_TRANSMISSION);
+                       CONFIG_ADD_LOGGING(S.logging.transmission, CONFIG_LOG_TRANSMISSION) |
                        CONFIG_ADD_LOGGING(S.logging.logid, CONFIG_LOG_LOGID);
     pConfig->priority1 = S.capabilities.priority1;
     pConfig->priority2 = S.capabilities.priority2;
